
I think that a brief diatribe on the subject of customization is in order:

AutoCAD needs to be customized - once one is aware of the possibilities
inherent in modifying and adding to the basic program, it becomes apparent
that it was designed and written without trying to add every concievable
feature in the expectation that each purchaser would modify the program and
add the commands and functions he required.  A simple and very powerful
solution to an otherwise unmanageable variety of needs.

Unfortunately having realized this brilliant conception almost no mention
was made of the intent or of the possibilities.  AutoCAD is sold and used in
its packaged form, and at best its potential is ignored.  The menu is
sometimes slightly altered to put the stamp of the corporate ego on it, or
to use the valuable upper areas to insert a small variety of blocks;
occasionally the more generic commands are inflated from one square to two
in case someone can't hit a half-inch square at point blank.
And there it sits.

What should Autodesk have done?  I can't fault them for wanting to supply a
program which could be run without diving head first into customizing, but
the standard menu could have been better laid out, and they might have
included a couple of different ones just to point out that variations were
in fact possible.  They also could have revamped it in one of the later
releases, assuming that they aren't convinced that it's already perfect.
Certainly it's not practical to design one interface to suit all of the
things one can do with Autocad, but then, as mentioned, that wasn't really
the idea, though it has inevitably become the accepted state of affairs.

The obvious point, of course, is that they should be pushing customization
to the hilt, not just burying it in the back of the manual: Why don't they
offer special sessions to their dealers so that they can teach the end users
how to customize Autocad, put a more elaborate and forceful section in the
manual on modifications (or even make a special pamphlet to include with it
which operators could copy and keep at their desks - quite often the manuals
are too sacred for the users to actually touch), mention the point in their
advertising, ask for comments and ideas about the matter in their
questionnaires, and generally try to get people to use Autocad rather than
just tolerate it.

A few related points:

      1.  If you have version 1 of this package you will recognize portions
of this note as having been attached to the entry for SL.LSP in the index
file.  I have tried to offer some constructive suggestions here rather than
just rant as I did last time.

      2.  I seem to criticise Autocad a lot.  You may ascribe my ire to
irritation at at the few rough spots in what is otherwise such a well
designed product.  As for the manual, it should recieve the "Shining example
amid an endless sea of drivel" award - it is about the only well written
instruction book I have ever seen included with a program.  Those who
criticise it for not being a tutorial are missing the point.

      3.  Speaking of customizing, I can understand the urge to protect
programming secrets, but the ability to encrypt Lisp files is, in my
experience, most often used by zealous supervisors to prevent operators from
making legitimate changes.  Forgive me if I discount as paranoid fantasies
the claims by management that their people can't be trusted to not wreck the
files/network/organizational structure/coffee machine etc.  If your
employees are really destructive idiots rather than intelligent people
reduced to a comatose state by victorian working conditions, why do you have
them doing work which is so important that it must be protected from them?
  In any case, I don't really think that the hard part of Lisp programming
is writing the thing, it's having the idea in the first place, which is a
bit more difficult to encrypt.  Most pirated software is lifted whole for its
abilities, not dissected as a source of programming techniques.
  If this feature is absolutely essential why not alter the format of these
files so you can insert one into one another: If I could put an encrypted
file inside Acad.lsp and have it function, and if I could tack a file of my
own onto the end of an encrypted Acad.lsp then I would be able to manipulate
the system as was intended without the danger of my seeing anyone's amazing
secret routines.  Alternatively if Autocad would refuse to accept an
encrypted Acad.lsp file then encrypted files could still be loaded with
(load "FILENAME") while leaving Acad.lsp free for the user to put his own
valued routines in.
  (Workaround for the adventurous: rename Acad.lsp to Crud.lsp.  Make a new
Acad.lsp containing the line: (LOAD "C:/ACAD/CRUD").  Then put the routines
you really need in the new Acad.lsp.)
 If Autodesk wants to sell their product to as many people as possible they
are going to have to avoid adding features which allow the misguided to chop
sections out of it: You don't get much advertising mileage or many repeat
sales out of "You can modify this program so that it's damn nearly useless."

      4.  The people who set up Autocad systems can't be held completely
responsible for not seeing all the possibilities of the thing, or for
adopting the traditional stance that employees are little better than
baboons, but it would not be unreasonable to ask them to innovate a little.
If you don't want to get into customizing then at least encourage your
operators to try new ideas.  Maybe ask Autodesk or your dealer to hold a
training session.

      5.  Before anyone uses the usual comeback, it takes very little time
to adapt to changes (specifically a new menu) if they are well designed.
Unless you are actually a turkey masquerading as a computer operator.



Finally a few suggestions on customization for the converted:

      1.  Most of AutoCAD's various menus and setting files can be changed
from within a drawing - in many cases this is the easiest way: You don't
have to quit, make changes, start again, test and repeat: you just shell
out, edit, and keep going.  I routinely add macros to the pulldowns while in
a drawing, and even this simple ability makes work much easier: one can
automate repetitive tasks with little more work than entering them once.
The process makes a pleasant if brief break from the routine of work and
keeps ones mind from going completely numb; also it makes the remaining work
easier and less tedious and saves a lot of the time spent on it.  See the
notes on building a menu editor into the pulldowns in Sample.pgp.

      2.  The digitizer menu is ripe for revision: why not put the most used
commands at the top where they aren't covered by the puck when it is on the
pointing area (leaving your arm hanging off into space), delete all the
functions you never use, and fill the space with Lisp routines or variants
on Change and Layer commands and various styles, heights and registrations
of text and the many other endlessly repeated commands which you are deathly
sick of typing in.

      3.  If you aren't using Lisp...well, you're reading this so you
probably are.  If not, you're running AutoCAD at about 10% of what it's
capable of.  The Lisp manual claims not to be a tutorial but that's how I
got started; I would caution the novice that the more common pitfalls of
each command are not documented: you have to be doggedly patient.  I would
also note that the world needs a Lisp manual documenting for each command
what it does, the syntax (so far so good), common errors to watch out for,
and situations where it will be useful.  There may be a book like this, but
I have yet to find it amidst the mountain of "Here: copy what we did" manuals.

      4.  Customization IS easy, I can't stress the point too heavily.
Everything you need to know is in the manual, and you can experiment
fearlessly if you make a few backup files.  You can't hurt AutoCAD.
As mentioned in the file Sample.pgp, it is worth building an "Edit Menu"
selection into the pulldown menus; you will find that it gets used
constantly - anything you repeat more than twice is worth putting into a
pulldown, and not too many more than that make it a candidate for the
tablet.  Get into the habit of consciously noticing what you do repeatedly
that could be made into a single command and put somewhere in the menu
structure.  And modify and replot the digitizer overlay: I like to make a
new one about every two weeks, usually there are enough new things I want to
put on it by then.  Also a bit of ornamentation and (if you have access to a
pen plotter) some colour are nice: I spend several hours a day looking at
the digitizer and would rather not have to stare at the usual tattered
grey rag of electrostatic plotter paper.

